WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.640
Now I'm talking about the harsh realities
of the

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.280
assemblies and how the de Bruijn graphs
faces these realities.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.220
During the course of the discussion,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:16.020
we have made some ridiculous unrealistic
assumptions.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.650
Like, we assume that we have perfect
coverage of the genome by reads.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.700
That means at each position on the genome,
we have a read generated at

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.010
that position.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.120
Secondly, we assume that reads are
error-free.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.190
Thirdly, we assume that multiplicities of
k-mers are known.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:40.500
And also we assume that the distance between
reads within read-pairs were

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.800
known exactly.
But in reality, these

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:51.050
assumptions do not hold.
We don't have

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.404
perfect coverage of genome by reads.
Reads are error-prone.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.520
The multiplicity of k-mers are not known,
either.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.640
The distance between reads, within
read-pairs, are inexact.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.520
And there are many other problems.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.080
But let's go to the first unrealistic
assumption, perfect coverage.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.370
Here we have the genome on the top, and
here are the four reads.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:22.260
Clearly that we don't have the perfect
coverage in this case,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.950
and it violates the key assumption of the de
Bruijn graph.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:32.200
So, what we can do is to break the reads
into shorter k-mers.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.770
And after this operation,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.570
the key assumption of the de Bruijn graph
holds.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.260
What is next?

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.430
About the erroneous reads.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:47.610
This is an erroneous reads.
By breaking

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:53.075
an erroneous read into a series of k-mers, we
also generate

00:01:53.075 --> 00:01:54.867
false k-mers.

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.272
And how these

00:01:56.272 --> 00:01:58.110
false k-mers

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:04.222
appear in the graph.
Well, if k-mers were exact, then for any

00:02:04.222 --> 00:02:11.520
region of the genome, the de Bruijn
graph would look like this.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:18.700
But when we introduce incorrect k-mers into
the graph, we create bubble.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.080
Imagine, for an assembly data set usually
we have

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.832
millions of incorrect k-mers, and they form
a lot of

00:02:28.832 --> 00:02:34.380
bubbles.
So the challenge here is to find

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:40.120
out how the genome traverses this graph,
(depicting by the color line).

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.370
Researchers have developed algorithms for.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.620
removing these bubbles and simplify the
graph.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.410
But should the graph look very simple
after this step?

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.869
Well, this is the graph of a bacteria genome
after simplification.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:08.500
And now, it is time to ask and answer the
question, who are these people?

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.420
Pavel, are you still here?

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.080
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:16.150
Can you answer the question?
&gt;&gt; So, these people.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:17.870
are not criminals.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.600
These are scientists and entrepreneurs who
made their

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.370
genomes have been available a few years ago and
started the

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.000
personalized genomics revolution.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.530
And today this personalized genomic
sequencing efforts are in full

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.600
bloom, and we will soon expand sequencing
to millions of individuals.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.960
At the same time.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.400
there are efforts to sequence thousands of
genomes and we

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.170
will soon have nearly over a million
genomes, for example, sequenced.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.770
Simultaneously, with this there are
enormous effort to sequence cancer

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:56.120
genomes and understand what changes in the
genome lead to cancer.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.532
And at the heart of this,

00:03:57.532 --> 00:03:59.418
genomic revolution are by

00:03:59.418 --> 00:04:00.550
young

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.900
mathematicians like Son who has been
wroking for two

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.670
decades now to develop new algorithmic
ideas for genome sequencing.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.560
You may ask, well why in the world we
started by making some

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.840
ridiculous assumptions that Son had to
address

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.180
in the last segment of this presentation.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.170
So what's a good reason for this?

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.890
Without this assumption, we wouldn't be
able to present the main

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.630
ideas of existent genome sequencing
algorithms.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.530
And there's actually a common saying in
bioinformatics, to

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:37.750
simplify the problem to the point that it
becomes ridiculous,

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.020
solve this problem, which means to develop

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.480
the key idea for solving a ridiculous
problem.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.925
And then overcome some ridiculous
assumptions.

00:04:45.925 --> 00:04:48.970
Something that Son described in the last
segment.

